      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To find the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line given by the equation \\(2x + 3y = 6\\), we first need to determine the slope of the given line. We can start by solving for \\(y\\) in terms of \\(x\\).\n\nStep 1. Rewrite the equation \\(2x + 3y = 6\\) in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\), where \\(m\\) is the slope and \\(b\\) is the y-intercept.\n\\[2x + 3y = 6\\]\n\\[3y = -2x + 6\\]\n\\[y = -\\frac{2}{3}x + 2\\]\n\nFrom the slope-intercept form, we see that the slope of the original line is \\(-\\frac{2}{3}\\). \n\nStep 2. The slope of the line that is perpendicular to another line is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the original line. Therefore, to find the slope \\(m_{\\text{perpendicular}}\\) of the perpendicular line, we calculate:\n\n\\[m_{\\text{perpendicular}} = -\\frac{1}{m_{\\text{original}}}\\]\n\nSubstituting the slope of the original line \\(m_{\\text{original}} = -\\frac{2}{3}\\), we get:\n\n\\[m_{\\text{perpendicular}} = -\\frac{1}{-\\frac{2}{3}} = \\frac{3}{2}\\]\n\nThus, the slope of the line that is perpendicular to the line \\(2x + 3y = 6\\) is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{3}{2}}\\).",
